WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:13.000
There are two countries in the world that
are often held up as examples of what not

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:14.000
to do.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.860
Two countries that for a long time seemed
able to conquer the world but which have been

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:22.250
undergoing chronic crisis for decades. Crises
from which they cannot escape.

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:29.990
Do you know which countries I'm talking about?

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:34.430
One is Argentina, that’s easy. The other
is 18,000 km away, and is none other than

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:40.520
Japan.

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:43.780
Of course this doesn’t mean that these two
countries are the same, not at all.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:48.120
The fact is that whenever a developed country
has problems, Japan always appears as an example

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:52.789
of what not to do.

00:00:52.789 --> 00:01:01.219
Europe’s Remedies for Japanization. Weak
growth and slowing inflation could have Europeans

00:01:01.219 --> 00:01:03.750
afraid of repeating Japan’s mistakes. WSJ)

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:07.670
Perhaps, folks, the person who experienced
the most negative aspects of Japan’s decline

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:22.149
in recent decades was Emperor Akihito, who
abdicated the throne on April 30th, 2019.

00:01:22.149 --> 00:01:26.310
Allow me to explain. Japan’s emperor has
no real power, and Akihito’s reign was a

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:27.619
particularly unlucky one.

00:01:27.619 --> 00:01:35.210
When he came to the throne in 1989, Japan
seemed to be ready to take on the world.

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:39.509
That same year, Japanese company, Sony, got
a major foothold in Hollywood by buying Columbia

00:01:39.509 --> 00:01:40.509
Pictures;

00:01:40.509 --> 00:01:43.880
Mitsubishi’s real estate division got the
Rockefeller Center itself, Japanese companies

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:47.770
were investing everywhere and all forecasts
indicated that the Japanese economy would

00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:51.539
surpass even the US’s economy within a few
years.

00:01:51.539 --> 00:01:55.509
However, in just three years everything fell
apart.

00:01:55.509 --> 00:01:59.850
The Japanese bubble exploded, the Tokyo stock
market collapsed and the crisis spread throughout

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:08.560
the Japanese financial system. The years of
strong economic growth had ended forever.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:13.190
Since 1991, the average economic growth has
been only 0.9% per year,

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:17.010
public debt has increased from 50 to 236%
of the GDP;

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:20.930
It was China that snatched second place in
the largest economies in the world,

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:25.230
Japanese companies lost ground and Japan was
left out of the top positions in the great

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:26.230
internet revolution

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:32.859
The cost of this decline? Strenuous working
hours of 12 hours or more are still common,

00:02:32.859 --> 00:02:37.420
Japanese workers barely enjoy ten days of
vacation a year and wages are even lower than

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:39.440
three decades ago in real terms.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:43.140
And that’s not all. The last three decades
haven’t only been bad from an economic point

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:49.780
of view, they have also been very rocky in
political terms. Japan has had 17 prime ministers

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:55.560
from five different political parties in the
last thirty years. That’s insane!

00:02:55.560 --> 00:03:01.569
And as if this weren’t enough, during Akihito’s
years the birthrate has been low resulting

00:03:01.569 --> 00:03:05.680
in an aging population and the country has
faced disasters such as the tsunami and the

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:13.310
Fukushima nuclear accident of 2011. Not to
mention the North Korean threat...

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:17.010
Now you understand why we say that Emperor
Akihito had bad luck?

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:20.439
But hold on a second.
When we think of Japan it’s not just this

00:03:20.439 --> 00:03:24.300
crisis that we think about. There’s manga,
sushi, car brands like Toyota so:

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:28.939
Are things really so bad in Japan?

00:03:28.939 --> 00:03:35.719
What if I told you that things haven’t been
as negative as they seem? What if Japan were

00:03:35.719 --> 00:03:38.060
somehow starting to shine again?

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:41.239
In this video we decided to look at Japan’s
best features.

00:03:41.239 --> 00:03:46.000
And also, at a social revolution that is stealthily
taking place and that could set a benchmark

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:49.340
or at least provide an experiment for the
rest of the world.

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:57.719
Listen up.

00:03:57.719 --> 00:04:02.019
THE BEST SIDE

00:04:02.019 --> 00:04:06.969
Japan has been having a bad time for three
decades, it’s been stuck in a kind of stagnation

00:04:06.969 --> 00:04:08.029
that it cannot overcome.

00:04:08.029 --> 00:04:11.939
Yet despite this, their numbers are still
pretty good.

00:04:11.939 --> 00:04:19.769
Obviously, it won’t surprise you when I
say that Japan is still a very wealthy country,

00:04:19.769 --> 00:04:20.769
we all know that.

00:04:20.769 --> 00:04:25.760
But, the question is, how rich or, rather,
how prosperous is the country of the rising

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:27.860
sun?

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:35.360
Well, folks, we could say that Japan is still
one of the top 20 countries in the world where

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:38.220
households have the most income after taxes.

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:45.340
With $ 28,641 USD, Japan is at the level of
countries such as Denmark, Finland or the

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:51.760
Netherlands and is above the United Kingdom,
New Zealand or Ireland.

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:59.950
And it’s also one of the five or six countries
where, on average, families have the most

00:04:59.950 --> 00:05:09.250
financial assets once their debts have been
deducted.

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:13.170
It also has one the highest life expectancies,
is one of the safest countries and has one

00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:17.950
of the best educational levels. In the PISA
index Japan is just behind Singapore.

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:22.660
In R &amp; D, research and development, more than
$150 billion USD are invested each year, ranking

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:27.000
them third highest country in levels of spending
and the fifth highest when you compare the

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:31.750
amount spent to their GDP. Japan comes in
just below South Korea, Israel, Sweden and

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:32.750
Taiwan.

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:38.280
And, folks, even though they have the highest
public debt in the world, no less than 237%

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:47.280
of the GDP, it really isn’t as serious as
it seems.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:53.900
The Japanese government has several debts,
yes, but it also has a lot of financial assets,

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:59.410
and if you take these in to account, its net
debt is decreased to approximately 150%.

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:04.110
You shouldn’t only count what you owe but
also what you have.

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:11.300
And that's not all. The government’s main
creditor is the Central Bank... which in a

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:15.230
way means that the entire debt has already
been shared among the Japanese for the last

00:06:15.230 --> 00:06:16.230
three decades:

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:19.270
For example, with a lower financial return,
with a good part of the capital going into

00:06:19.270 --> 00:06:23.860
public hands due to Central Bank purchases
of assets and with less real wages.

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:27.900
In any case, the entire debt is in the hands
of the Japanese so to speak. Japan has no

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:31.510
external debt, it isn’t a debtor, but quite
the opposite.

00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:37.940
Japan, China and Germany world's top creditors.
Japan kept its position as the world’s largest

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:41.800
creditor nation for the 22nd straight year
last year. South China Post Morning)

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:45.730
And unlike other countries, their companies
aren’t linked to the debt either.

00:06:45.730 --> 00:06:51.450
Look no further than the Tokyo Stock Market,
the 4th largest stock market in the world,

00:06:51.450 --> 00:06:56.110
where 60% of the companies have no net debt.

00:06:56.110 --> 00:07:00.020
Seen this way, in spite of all of the country’s
problems – because they do have them – things

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:03.290
don’t look so bad, do they?

00:07:03.290 --> 00:07:09.720
And what’s more, in recent years, between
2007 and 2017, a curious development has taken

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:10.720
place.

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:14.050
See, the Japanese economy is barely growing,
that’s a fact, but as its population is

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:18.030
shrinking and its economy has no inflation,
the real growth per capita, which ultimately

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:22.530
determines the level of well-being, has risen
to equal the growth rate of the US and is

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:26.000
ranked above countries such as France or the
United Kingdom.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:27.000
Surprised?

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:33.260
You shouldn’t be. This has been largely
driven by the Asian continent’s economic

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:34.260
explosion.

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:37.960
After all, Japan has neighbors such as China,
Vietnam and India, where, for example, Suzuki,

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:44.260
a Japanese car company, accounts for just
over 50% of the entire Indian car market.

00:07:44.260 --> 00:07:48.840
We’re talking about billions of potential
consumers.

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:54.280
Want another example? Look at what has happened
with tourism.

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:57.950
You see, traditionally Japan has had very
few tourists. Mainly because of its distance

00:07:57.950 --> 00:07:59.370
from other developed countries.

00:07:59.370 --> 00:08:02.280
However in recent years, it’s become quite
the theme park.

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:05.740
Japan has the largest growing tourist arrival
rate in the world.

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:09.150
Within a short time, and largely thanks to
Chinese visitors, the number of foreign tourists

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:15.180
has gone from just over six million in 2011
to over 31 million in 2018. And forecasts

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:19.780
say that this figure could exceed 60, 60 million
by 2030.

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:26.150
Obviously all this means a lot of new jobs.
But its population is the oldest in the world

00:08:26.150 --> 00:08:31.170
and is, in fact, reduced every year. So, how
can they fill all these new positions?

00:08:31.170 --> 00:08:35.320
Folks, that’s where the social revolution
promoted by the Prime Minister, Shinzo Abe,

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:36.340
comes into play.

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:40.659
Listen up, because Japan is becoming an experiment
on how to deal with demographic aging. And

00:08:40.659 --> 00:08:42.870
I think their solution will surprise you

00:08:42.870 --> 00:08:53.430
(THE SOCIAL REVOLUTION)

00:08:53.430 --> 00:08:58.450
Okay, something is very clear for Japan; in
such an old country where the population is

00:08:58.450 --> 00:09:02.980
expected to decrease significantly during
the upcoming decades, only productivity and

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:06.160
investment in technology and robots can save
them.

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:10.310
But, friends, this takes time... and of course
to be able to invest as much as they need,

00:09:10.310 --> 00:09:13.430
they have to become a prosperous economy,
so the question is:

00:09:13.430 --> 00:09:18.480
How can a stagnating economy recover and keep
public finances under control while the population

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:23.300
ages at full speed – especially when an
aging population means in terms of pensions

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:26.510
and health spending?

00:09:26.510 --> 00:09:32.560
Well, the prime minister, Shinzo Abe believes
that it’s essential to increase labor participation

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:36.180
in the short term, raise the percentage of
people who work, which is precisely what is

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:38.290
happening in Japan:

00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:49.509
But... Have you noticed? How are they managing
to increase the number of workers in a country

00:09:49.509 --> 00:09:55.230
if the population is both shrinking and aging
at a rapid pace? What the hell is Japan doing?

00:09:55.230 --> 00:10:03.750
Well, folks, the answer is that Shinzo Abe
is leading a social revolution in Japan.

00:10:03.750 --> 00:10:06.069
Obviously with less demanding jobs and shorter
shifts.

00:10:06.069 --> 00:10:11.069
This has led more than half of all Japanese
men aged between 65 and 70 to go back to work.

00:10:11.069 --> 00:10:15.680
More than double the average in the OECD countries,
which includes almost all of the most developed

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:16.680
countries.

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:29.970
Have you seen the movie “The Intern” with
Robert de Niro? Well... something like that.

00:10:29.970 --> 00:10:33.839
On the other hand, the government is also
promoting a historic increase in female labor

00:10:33.839 --> 00:10:37.120
participation, which in Japan has not traditionally
been very high.

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:41.329
Japan is experiencing a huge transformation.
The number of women working and occupying

00:10:41.329 --> 00:10:43.149
leadership positions is growing.

00:10:43.149 --> 00:10:47.639
This change has been driven by what has become
known as the "Womenomics", a policy that for

00:10:47.639 --> 00:10:52.399
example has increased day care services, incentivized
part-time contracts or encouraged companies

00:10:52.399 --> 00:10:55.230
to adjust their schedules to the needs of
women with children.

00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:59.329
That is how Japan has become one of the countries
with the greatest percentage of working women

00:10:59.329 --> 00:11:00.680
today.

00:11:00.680 --> 00:11:05.620
However, the truth is that despite all these
measures, the Japanese economy still needs

00:11:05.620 --> 00:11:09.220
many more workers. In fact, there are so many
vacancies that there are currently more than

00:11:09.220 --> 00:11:11.670
150 jobs for every 100 candidates.

00:11:11.670 --> 00:11:16.279
Yes, you heard that right. There are more
jobs available than candidates. Many more.

00:11:16.279 --> 00:11:20.329
That’s why, in recent years, Shinzo Abe
has been demolishing what has so far been

00:11:20.329 --> 00:11:25.170
a taboo for Japanese society.

00:11:25.170 --> 00:11:32.459
(THE ARRIVAL OF NEW BLOOD)

00:11:32.459 --> 00:11:36.740
Since time immemorial Japan has been one of
the countries most closed to immigration in

00:11:36.740 --> 00:11:39.700
the world. We already talked about this a
while ago on VisualPolitik.

00:11:39.700 --> 00:11:44.360
In Japan, the immigrant population accounts
for only 1.8% of the total. One of the lowest

00:11:44.360 --> 00:11:49.720
percentages, if not the lowest, in the entire
developed world.

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:56.449
However, that is changing.

00:11:56.449 --> 00:12:02.040
Japan to Asia: Give us your young, your skilled,
your eager workers”. Nikkei Asian Review)

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:06.269
Since Shinzo Abe came to power in 2012 the
number of foreigners has doubled.

00:12:06.269 --> 00:12:14.720
And that won’t be the end of it. In December
of 2018, Japan approved new laws to boost

00:12:14.720 --> 00:12:16.459
the arrival of immigrants.

00:12:16.459 --> 00:12:21.089
We need to break free of the conventional
thinking. I will create a Japan that can attract

00:12:21.089 --> 00:12:23.430
great talent from around the world". Shinzo
Abe.)

00:12:23.430 --> 00:12:27.940
Folks, the new regulations make allowances
for two new status for the foreign population.

00:12:27.940 --> 00:12:31.399
On the one hand, a very appealing status to
attract the most qualified workers, who can

00:12:31.399 --> 00:12:36.370
settle in Japan, practically indefinitely,
with their families.

00:12:36.370 --> 00:12:40.510
On the other hand, a new status designed so
industries can bring less qualified workers.

00:12:40.510 --> 00:12:44.450
Here the conditions are more limited, but
even so the new regulation seeks to eliminate

00:12:44.450 --> 00:12:48.490
all the problems and the bad conditions that
these foreign workers have typically suffered,

00:12:48.490 --> 00:12:53.990
something that we already talked about in
this other video.

00:12:53.990 --> 00:13:01.480
In any case, folks, Japan has many, many problems,
we have talked about them a lot of times and

00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:05.529
we will do so again in the future. But there
are also reasons it can remain one of the

00:13:05.529 --> 00:13:07.230
richest countries in the world.

00:13:07.230 --> 00:13:10.779
So I really hope you enjoyed this video, please
hit like if you did, and don’t forget to

00:13:10.779 --> 00:13:13.860
subscribe for brand new videos. Don’t forget
to check out our friends at the Reconsider

00:13:13.860 --> 00:13:18.579
Media Podcast - they provided the voiceover
in this episode. Also, this channel is possible

00:13:18.579 --> 00:13:22.040
because of Patreon, and our patrons on that
platform. Please consider joining them and

00:13:22.040 --> 00:13:26.470
supporting our mission of providing independent
political coverage. And as always, I’ll

00:13:26.470 --> 00:13:27.189
see you in the next video.

